"""Shorter Why lines (answer pages must fit 21 pages). Applied by lib.Unit.save()."""
SHORT = {
 'L3U07-S01': 'Area is the number of squares: A 8, B 6, C 6. A is 2 × (4 + 2) = 12 and C is 2 × (3 + 2) = 10. B fits a 4 by 2 box (12), and the dent adds 1 cm on each side: 14. B and C have the same area but different perimeters.',
 'L3U02-T03': 'The middle number is in 4 lines (the middle row and column and both diagonals). Adding those 4 lines counts the middle 4 times and every other number once, which is 3 totals + 3 × the middle. So the total is 3 × 210 = 630. Then A = 630 - 120 - 270 = 240 and E = 630 - 120 - 210 = 300.',
 'L3U05-G02': 'Each clue is 2 short of a full group: 1 left in 3s, 2 left in 4s and 3 left in 5s. So the number is 2 less than a number in the 3, 4 and 5 times tables. The first one is 60, so the number is 58.',
 'L3U10-G02': '(a) Put 1 coin on each side. If the scale tips, the light coin is on the high side. If it balances, it is the coin left out: 1 weighing. (b) Weigh 3 coins against 3 coins to find the group of 3 with the light coin. Then use (a): 2 weighings.',
 'L3U08-S08': '(a) Each corner of a rectangle is a right angle: 4. (b) The 2 long sides are parallel and the 2 short sides are parallel: 2 pairs. (c) 2 right angles would make 2 parallel sides, which never meet to close a triangle: at most 1. (d) The sides of a square meet at right angles.',
 'L3U06-G02': 'In eighths a half is 4, a quarter is 2 and an eighth is 1. 6 pieces must make 8 eighths. 2 quarters (4) and 4 eighths (4) make 8. A half would leave 5 pieces to make 4 eighths, which cannot be done.',
 'L3U02-S13': 'Greatest sum: the 2 greatest digits go in the hundreds places, the next 2 in the tens places, the last 2 in the ones places: 800 + 700 + 50 + 40 + 3 + 2 = 1595. Smallest sum: do the same with the smallest digits first: 200 + 300 + 40 + 50 + 7 + 8 = 605.',
 'L3U07-T03': '(a) 16 + 9 = 25 cm². (b) The figure has no dents, so its perimeter equals its box, 7 cm by 4 cm: 2 × (7 + 4) = 22 cm. (c) The new figure fits a 4 by 7 box and has no dents: 22 cm again.',
 'L3U10-T02': 'If Ben did it, Aisha and Wei both tell the truth: 2 truths. If Wei did it, Ben and Aisha both tell the truth: 2 truths. If Aisha did it, only Wei tells the truth: exactly 1. So Aisha did it.',
 'L3U10-S08': 'The hundreds digit is 1, 2 or 3. The ones digit is 2, 4 or 6 and the tens digit is greater. With 1: tens + ones = 8, so 6 and 2. With 2: 7, so 5 and 2. With 3: 6, so 4 and 2. The numbers are 162, 252 and 342.',
 'L3U05-G01': '(a) 3 + 5 = 8, and 8 ÷ 7 leaves 1. (b) 3 + 3 = 6, which is smaller than 7. (c) 5 + 5 = 10, and 10 ÷ 8 leaves 2. Test with real numbers: 10 + 5 = 15 leaves 1; 20 leaves 6; 26 ÷ 8 leaves 2.',
 'L3U08-S03': 'F has parallel arms and a stem at right angles to them. V has neither. N has 2 parallel upright lines, but its sloping line is not at a right angle. L has 2 lines that meet at a right angle.',
 'L3U09-S11': 'The short hand shows the hour and the long hand the minutes. A: long hand at 2 is 10 minutes, short hand just past 4. B: long hand at 9 is 45 minutes. C: long hand at 5 is 25 minutes.',
 'L3U07-S10': 'A square that touches the figure on only 1 side adds 2 to the perimeter: A and B give 12 cm. C fits into the corner and touches 2 sides: it hides 2 cm and adds 2 cm, so the perimeter stays 10 cm.',
 'L3U08-G02': '(a) Two lines that are perpendicular to the same line stay the same distance apart and never meet: they are parallel. (b) A line across 2 parallel lines makes the same angle with both, so yes.',
 'L3U01-T03': 'Numbers that start with 1 or 2 are below 2500, because the second digit is at most 4. Only those that start with 3 or 4 are greater: 6 + 6 = 12. The list begins 1234, 1243, 1324, 1342, 1423, 1432, so the 7th is 2134.',
 'L3U03-T02': '49 = 7 × 7, so row 2 and column 1 are 7. 42 = 6 × 7 gives row 1 = 6. 48 = 6 × 8 gives column 2 = 8. 63 = 7 × 9 gives column 3 = 9. 72 = 8 × 9 gives row 3 = 8. A = 54, B = 56, C = 56, D = 64.',
 'L3U07-S07': 'a = 8 - 4 = 4 cm and b = 6 - 3 = 3 cm. Area: a 4 by 6 rectangle and a 4 by 3 rectangle: 24 + 12 = 36 cm². Perimeter: push the steps out to a box 8 by 6: 2 × (8 + 6) = 28 cm.',
 'L3U02-T02': 'The smallest digit, 1, goes in the hundreds place. The next smallest, 3 and 5, go in the tens places. 6 and 8 go in the ones places. For example 136 + 58 = 194, and every such way gives 194.',
 'L3U02-G03': '99 = 9 × 11, 143 = 13 × 11, 121 = 11 × 11. A number with digits A and B is 10 A + B, and its reverse is 10 B + A. Together they are 11 × (A + B), always in the 11 times table.',
 'L3U07-S08': '(a) 6 + 4 + 2 = 12 cm². (b) Same as its 6 by 3 box: 2 × (6 + 3) = 18 cm. (c) The rectangle has 18 squares: 18 - 12 = 6 more. (d) 18 cm: filling the steps does not change the perimeter.',
 'L3U10-T03': 'Monday is 1 box, Tuesday 1 box and 15, Wednesday 2 boxes, Thursday 2 boxes take away 10. In all: 6 boxes + 5 = 185, so 1 box = 30. Monday 30, Tuesday 45, Wednesday 60, Thursday 50.',
 'L3U10-S04': 'Only Lily has a 4-letter name: orange. Kumar has neither apple nor banana, so grapes. Wei does not have the apple, so banana. Aisha has the apple.',
 'L3U08-S07': 'Turning right from north: east, south, west. (a) 1 turn: east. (b) 3 turns: west. (c) 2 turns left: west, then south. Two right angles either way face the opposite way: south.',
 'L3U02-S07': 'Middle row: 550 + 350 + 150 = 1050. Top row: 200 + 450 + A = 1050, so A = 400. Middle column: 450 + 350 + B = 1050, so B = 250. Bottom row: 300 + 250 + C = 1050, so C = 500.',
 'L3U08-S02': 'A is an L: 5 right angles, and its dent is greater than a right angle. B has 6 right angles and 2 dents. C has right angles only at its 2 left corners; its 2 sloping corners do not fit the checker.',
 'L3U02-S11': 'Turn it round: 3657 + 2845 = 6502. Or subtract by columns: 12 - 5 = 7, 10 - 1 - 4 = 5, 15 - 1 - 8 = 6, 6 - 1 - 2 = 3. The top number is 6502.',
 'L3U01-T02': 'The digit sets are 1, 1, 8 and 1, 2, 4 and 2, 2, 2. The set 1, 1, 8 makes 118, 181, 811: 3 numbers. The set 1, 2, 4 makes 6 numbers. The set 2, 2, 2 makes 222. 3 + 6 + 1 = 10.',
 'L3U07-G03': 'The side must divide both 12 and 10. 1 and 2 do; 3, 4, 5 and 6 each fail one of them. So the largest side is 2 m: 6 tiles along and 5 across, 30 tiles.',
 'L3U07-T01': 'Each rectangle is 8 cm by 4 cm: perimeter 24 cm, so 48 cm together. The square has 32 cm, and 48 - 32 = 16. The 8 cm cut appears on both pieces, adding 2 × 8 = 16 cm.',
 'L3U08-G03': '(a) 2 lines across and 2 lines up: 2 pairs of parallel lines. (b) 2 × 2 = 4 crossings, each with 4 right angles: 16. (c) 3 × 3 = 9 crossings, 9 × 4 = 36 right angles.',
 'L3U05-T02': 'Numbers with remainder 2 when divided by 5 end in 2 or 7. 67 = 66 + 1 (66 is in the 3 times table) and 67 = 64 + 3 (64 is in the 4 times table). No other number works.',
 'L3U08-T02': 'AB goes 2 across for every 1 up. A perpendicular line goes 1 across and 2 down. From C, that reaches the dot at 3 across and 4 up, then (4, 2), then the bottom row at R.',
 'L3U03-S08': '54 and 63 are both in the 9 times table: 54 = 9 × 6 and 63 = 9 × 7. So the row number is 9 and the columns are 6 and 7. Second row: 48 = 8 × 6 and 56 = 8 × 7, so the row number is 8.',
 'L3U03-G04': 'The last digits of 1 × 1 up to 9 × 9 are 1, 4, 9, 6, 5, 6, 9, 4, 1. Only the last digit matters, so a square can end only in 0, 1, 4, 5, 6 or 9. 2, 3, 7 and 8 never.',
 'L3U02-G02': 'The end bricks of the bottom row count once in the top brick and the 2 middle bricks 3 times each. So 200 + 3 X + 3 X + 440 = 3280, 6 X = 2640 and X = 440.',
 'L3U08-S09': '(a) That is what perpendicular means. (b) Parallel lines never meet. (c) A line across 2 rails of a ladder is perpendicular to both. (d) Lines can cross at any angle.',
 'L3U08-S05': 'AB goes 4 across and 2 up. R goes 4 across and 2 up too, so it never meets AB: parallel. P goes 2 across and 4 down, a right angle to AB: perpendicular.',
 'L3U05-S10': 'The pattern repeats every 4 letters. 30 ÷ 4 = 7 remainder 2, so the 30th letter is the 2nd of the pattern: B. 32 ÷ 4 = 8 with none left, so the 32nd is the last: D.',
 'L3U02-G01': 'Ones: C + C ends in B. Tens: B + B (+ carry) ends in A. Hundreds: A (+ carry) is C. Try C = 3: B = 6, carry 0. Tens: 6 + 6 = 12, so A = 2 and carry 1. 2 + 1 = 3 = C. No other digits work.',
 'L3U05-S08': 'Between 30 and 40: 6 × 5 = 30 and 6 × 6 = 36, so the numbers are 31 and 37. Test with 4: 31 ÷ 4 = 7 remainder 3 (right). 37 ÷ 4 = 9 remainder 1 (not right). The number is 31.',
 'L3U02-T01': 'Two 2-digit numbers make less than 200, so B = 1 and the answer starts 11. Ones: 1 + C ends in D. Tens: A + A + carry makes 11, so A = 5 with carry 1. Then 1 + C = 10, so C = 9, D = 0.',
 'L3U01-S06': '(a) Greatest digits first: 8752. (b) Smallest first, no 0 here: 2578. (c) The ones digit is 7 or 5. With 5 last, 8725 beats 8527. (d) With 7 last, 2587 beats 2785.',
 'L3U01-S07': 'The first digit is 3 or 5, never 0. After 3, the 5 goes in the hundreds, tens or ones place: 3005, 3050, 3500. After 5, the same: 5003, 5030, 5300. That is 6 numbers.',
 'L3U01-S08': '(a) Add 300 each time. (b) Subtract 250 each time. (c) Add 8: 30 + 8 = 38, 38 + 8 = 46.',
 'L3U01-S13': 'The first 7 is in the hundreds place: 700. The second is in the ones place: 7. 700 - 7 = 693.',
 'L3U01-T01': 'Only two sets of 4 different digits add up to 8: 0, 1, 2, 5 and 0, 1, 3, 4. Even numbers need an even last digit. Greatest even: 5210. Smallest: 1034.',
 'L3U01-T02': 'The digit sets are 1, 1, 8 and 1, 2, 4 and 2, 2, 2. Set 1, 1, 8 makes 118, 181, 811: 3 numbers. Set 1, 2, 4 makes 6 numbers. Set 2, 2, 2 makes 222. 3 + 6 + 1 = 10.',
 'L3U01-G01': 'Sum 4: first digit 4 gives 1 number (400), 3 gives 2 (310, 301), 2 gives 3, 1 gives 4: 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10. Sum 5, first digit 1 gives 5 numbers: 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 15.',
 'L3U01-G03': 'List the numbers that fit clue 1, then cross out those that fail clue 2, and so on. In the example, tens digit t gives 7 + 2t + t + (t + 1) = 16, so t = 2 and the number is 7423.',
 'L3U01-G04': 'The thousands digit is 3 and the tens digit is 0. If the ones digit is o, the hundreds digit is 2o, so 3 + 2o + o = 15 and o = 4. The hundreds digit is 8. The number is 3804.',
 'L3U01-C03': 'The first digit cannot be 0, so use 1. Then the rest in order: 0, 5, 6. The number is 1056.',
 'L3U02-S05': 'The check should give 5000, but 3342 + 1768 = 5110. So Siti is not right. 5000 - 1768 = 3232.',
 'L3U02-S08': '830 + P = 1580, so P = 750. Q + 270 = 750, so Q = 480.',
 'L3U02-S10': '(a) Try A = 3: 33 + 23 = 56. It works. (b) Two equal numbers add to 90, so each is 45. Then C = 4.',
 'L3U02-S13': 'Greatest sum: the 2 greatest digits go in the hundreds places, the next 2 in the tens, the last 2 in the ones: 800 + 700 + 50 + 40 + 3 + 2 = 1595. Smallest: do the same with the smallest digits: 200 + 300 + 40 + 50 + 7 + 8 = 605.',
 'L3U02-G03': '99 = 9 × 11, 143 = 13 × 11, 121 = 11 × 11. A number with digits A and B is 10 A + B and its reverse is 10 B + A. Together: 11 × (A + B), always in the 11 times table.',
 'L3U03-S08': '54 and 63 are both in the 9 times table: 54 = 9 × 6, 63 = 9 × 7. So the row is 9 and the columns are 6 and 7. Second row: 48 = 8 × 6 and 56 = 8 × 7, so the row is 8.',
 'L3U03-G02': 'The seats go 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19, 21. Row 8 has 21 seats. Pair them: 7 + 21 = 28, 9 + 19 = 28, 11 + 17 = 28, 13 + 15 = 28. 4 pairs: 4 × 28 = 112 seats.',
 'L3U04-G02': 'Red is 4 boxes and blue is 1 box. After selling 18 red, red is 1 box. So 3 boxes are 18 and 1 box is 6. At first there were 6 blue and 24 red: 30 balloons.',
 'L3U04-G03': 'In the example, take away the 12: 60 - 12 = 48 and 48 ÷ 2 = 24. Ali has 24 and Ben has 36.',
 'L3U04-C03': 'There are 6 equal boxes. 72 ÷ 6 = 12, so 1 box is 12. Siti has 5 boxes: 5 × 12 = 60.',
 'L3U05-S12': 'A number leaves remainder 3 when it is 3 more than a number in the 5 times table. 18 = 15 + 3, 23 = 20 + 3, 33 = 30 + 3, 48 = 45 + 3. 41 = 40 + 1 leaves remainder 1.',
 'L3U05-S13': '47 ÷ 4 = 11 remainder 3. 47 ÷ 5 = 9 remainder 2. 47 ÷ 6 = 7 remainder 5. 47 ÷ 7 = 6 remainder 5. 47 ÷ 8 = 5 remainder 7. The greatest remainder is 7, from 8.',
 'L3U05-T02': 'Numbers with remainder 2 when divided by 5 end in 2 or 7. 67 = 66 + 1 (66 is in the 3 times table) and 67 = 64 + 3 (64 is in the 4 times table). No other works.',
 'L3U05-G01': '(a) 3 + 5 = 8, and 8 ÷ 7 leaves 1. (b) 3 + 3 = 6, smaller than 7. (c) 5 + 5 = 10, and 10 ÷ 8 leaves 2. Test: 10 + 5 = 15 leaves 1; 20 leaves 6; 26 ÷ 8 leaves 2.',
 'L3U06-S04': '(a) Same bottom number: 5 parts are more than 3. (b) Thirds are larger pieces than quarters. (c) 2/4 = 4/8. (d) 3/4 = 9/12 and 5/6 = 10/12, and 9 is less than 10.',
 'L3U06-S08': '(a) 3/12 + 4/12 = 7/12. (b) 12/12 - 7/12 = 5/12. (c) 4/12 - 3/12 = 1/12.',
 'L3U06-S10': 'Multiply or divide the top and the bottom by the same number. (a) 4 × 3 = 12, so 3 × 3 = 9. (b) 5 × 2 = 10, so 2 × 2 = 4. (c) 1 × 5 = 5, so 2 × 5 = 10. (d) 6 ÷ 2 = 3, so 8 ÷ 2 = 4.',
 'L3U06-T03': '1/4 = 3/12 and 1/3 = 4/12. (a) 3/12 + 4/12 = 7/12. (b) 12/12 - 7/12 = 5/12 is left.',
 'L3U07-S02': '(a) Area 8 × 6 = 48. Perimeter 2 × (8 + 6) = 28. (b) Area 11 × 4 = 44. Perimeter 2 × (11 + 4) = 30.',
 'L3U07-S07': 'a = 8 - 4 = 4 cm and b = 6 - 3 = 3 cm. Area: a 4 by 6 rectangle and a 4 by 3 rectangle: 24 + 12 = 36 cm². Perimeter: push the steps out to an 8 by 6 box: 2 × (8 + 6) = 28 cm.',
 'L3U07-S08': '(a) 6 + 4 + 2 = 12 cm². (b) Same as its 6 by 3 box: 2 × (6 + 3) = 18 cm. (c) The rectangle has 18 squares: 18 - 12 = 6 more. (d) 18 cm: filling the steps keeps the perimeter.',
 'L3U07-S09': '(a) Mei’s perimeter is 2 × (10 + 2) = 24 cm, so Ben’s side is 24 ÷ 4 = 6 cm. (b) Ben 6 × 6 = 36 cm², Mei 10 × 2 = 20 cm². (c) 36 - 20 = 16 cm².',
 'L3U07-T01': 'Each rectangle is 8 cm by 4 cm: perimeter 24 cm, so 48 cm in all. The square has 32 cm, and 48 - 32 = 16. The 8 cm cut is on both pieces: 2 × 8 = 16 cm.',
 'L3U07-T03': '(a) 16 + 9 = 25 cm². (b) No dents, so the perimeter equals its 7 cm by 4 cm box: 2 × (7 + 4) = 22 cm. (c) A 4 by 7 box and no dents: 22 cm again.',
 'L3U08-L01': 'The second line is inside the corner of the checker, so the angle is smaller than a right angle.',
 'L3U08-S06': 'At each end of a rung, the rung and the rail make 2 right angles (above and below the rung). A rung has 2 ends: 4 right angles. 3 rungs: 3 × 4 = 12.',
 'L3U08-S09': '(a) That is what perpendicular means. (b) Parallel lines never meet. (c) A line across the 2 rails of a ladder is perpendicular to both. (d) Lines can cross at any angle.',
 'L3U08-T02': 'AB goes 2 across for every 1 up. A perpendicular line goes 1 across and 2 down. From C, that reaches the dot at 3 across, 4 up, then (4, 2), then the bottom row at R.',
 'L3U08-G03': '(a) 2 lines across and 2 lines up: 2 pairs. (b) 2 × 2 = 4 crossings, each with 4 right angles: 16. (c) 3 × 3 = 9 crossings, 9 × 4 = 36 right angles.',
 'L3U08-C01': 'The second line sticks out past the corner of the checker: the angle is greater than a right angle.',
 'L3U09-S01': '(a) 3 × 100 = 300. (b) 4 kg = 4000 g, and 4000 + 50 = 4050. (c) 5 ℓ = 5000 ml, 5000 + 300 = 5300. (d) 2000 g is 2 kg, 750 g left. (e) 300 cm is 3 m, 65 cm left.',
 'L3U09-S04': '2 ℓ 500 ml = 2500 ml. 2500 ÷ 400 = 6 remainder 100. He fills 6 cups, with 100 ml left.',
 'L3U09-S11': 'The short hand shows the hour, the long hand the minutes. A: long hand at 2 is 10 minutes, short hand just past 4. B: long hand at 9 is 45 minutes. C: at 5 is 25 minutes.',
 'L3U09-T03': 'The 2 notebooks cost $3 more than 2 pens in all (2 × $1.50). Take that away: $9 - $3 = $6 for 5 pens. A pen costs $6 ÷ 5 = $1.20. A notebook costs $1.20 + $1.50 = $2.70.',
 'L3U09-C03': '9:40 to 10:00 is 20 min. 10:00 to 11:00 is 1 h. 11:00 to 11:10 is 10 min. In all: 1 h 30 min.',
 'L3U10-S02': 'The labelled lines go up in 20s and a thin line is halfway between them, so each thin line is 10. A is 70, B is 110, C is 90. Together: 70 + 110 + 90 = 270.',
 'L3U10-S03': '(a) 3rd to 24th is 21 days, which is 3 weeks. So the 24th is a Tuesday again. (b) 3rd to 30th is 27 days = 3 weeks and 6 days. 6 days after Tuesday is Monday.',
 'L3U10-S06': 'Fix the first digit and list the rest in order: 369, 396, then 639, 693, then 936, 963. The even numbers end in 6: 396 and 936. 693 is only 7 from 700; the others are much farther.',
 'L3U10-S10': '2 juices cost 2 × 1.85 = 3.70. With the sandwich: 3.70 + 3.40 = 7.10. Change: 10.00 - 7.10 = 2.90.',
 'L3U10-T03': 'Monday is 1 box, Tuesday 1 box and 15, Wednesday 2 boxes, Thursday 2 boxes take away 10. In all: 6 boxes + 5 = 185, so 1 box = 30. Monday 30, Tuesday 45, Wednesday 60, Thursday 50.',
 'L3U10-G01': 'There are 16 small squares. A 2 by 2 square fits in 3 places across and 3 down: 9. A 3 by 3 square fits 2 across and 2 down: 4. There is 1 big square. 16 + 9 + 4 + 1 = 30.',
 'L3U10-G03': 'If all 8 coins were 10¢, they would be worth 80¢. They are worth 150¢, 70¢ more. Changing one 10¢ coin to a 20¢ coin adds 10¢, so 7 coins are 20¢ and 1 coin is 10¢.',
 'L3U01-G01': 'Sum 4: first digit 4 gives 1 number (400), 3 gives 2, 2 gives 3, 1 gives 4: 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10. Sum 5, first digit 1 gives 5 numbers: 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 15.',
 'L3U01-S05': '3 boxes are 3000 pins. 14 packs are 1400. 3000 + 1400 + 6 = 4406.',
 'L3U01-S06': '(a) Greatest digits first: 8752. (b) Smallest first, no 0 here: 2578. (c) The ones digit is 7 or 5. 5 last: 8725 beats 8527. (d) 7 last: 2587 beats 2785.',
 'L3U01-S07': 'The first digit is 3 or 5, never 0. After 3, the 5 goes in the hundreds, tens or ones place: 3005, 3050, 3500. After 5, the same: 5003, 5030, 5300. 6 numbers.',
 'L3U01-T02': 'The digit sets are 1, 1, 8 and 1, 2, 4 and 2, 2, 2. Set 1, 1, 8 makes 118, 181, 811: 3 numbers. Set 1, 2, 4 makes 6. Set 2, 2, 2 makes 222. 3 + 6 + 1 = 10.',
 'L3U02-G02': 'The end bricks of the bottom row count once in the top brick and the 2 middle bricks 3 times each. So 200 + 6 X + 440 = 3280, 6 X = 2640 and X = 440.',
 'L3U02-G04': 'In the example: 800 - 370 = 430, 430 - 180 = 250, 370 - 250 = 120.',
 'L3U02-S01': '(a) Ones 6 + 1 = 7 and tens 4 + 5 = 9 are fine. Hundreds: ? + 2 = 5, so ? is 3. (b) Ones: 9 + 5 = 14, carry 1. Tens: ? + 3 + 1 ends in 0, so ? is 6.',
 'L3U02-S04': 'First add what he used: 2475 + 3150 = 5625. Then 8000 - 5625 = 2375.',
 'L3U02-S09': 'Ones: 2 + 6 = 8. Tens: 5 + ? ends in 4, so ? is 9, carry 1. Hundreds: ? + 4 + 1 ends in 1, so ? is 6, carry 1. Thousands: 3 + 2 + 1 = 6.',
 'L3U02-S13': 'Greatest sum: the 2 greatest digits go in the hundreds places, the next 2 in the tens, the last 2 in the ones: 800 + 700 + 50 + 40 + 3 + 2 = 1595. Smallest: 200 + 300 + 40 + 50 + 7 + 8 = 605.',
 'L3U02-S14': '(a) 1875 + 2430 = 4305, 4305 + 1790 = 6095. (b) 2430 - 1790 = 640.',
 'L3U03-G02': 'The seats go 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19, 21. Row 8 has 21 seats. Pair them: 7 + 21 = 28, 9 + 19 = 28, 11 + 17 = 28, 13 + 15 = 28. 4 × 28 = 112 seats.',
 'L3U03-G04': 'The last digits of 1 × 1 up to 9 × 9 are 1, 4, 9, 6, 5, 6, 9, 4, 1. Only the last digit matters, so a square ends only in 0, 1, 4, 5, 6 or 9. Never 2, 3, 7 or 8.',
 'L3U03-S01': 'The answers go up by 8. 8 × 4 = 32, 8 × 6 = 48 and 8 × 9 = 72.',
 'L3U03-S05': 'The 7 times table between 50 and 80: 56, 63, 70, 77. Their digits add up to 11, 9, 7 and 14. Only 63 has digits that add up to 9.',
 'L3U03-S12': 'Mei has 6 × 8 = 48 stickers. Ben has 7 × 7 = 49. Ben has 1 more.',
 'L3U04-C03': 'There are 6 equal boxes. 72 ÷ 6 = 12. Siti has 5 boxes: 5 × 12 = 60.',
 'L3U04-G01': 'Lily is 1 box. Kumar is 1 box and 12. Aisha is 2 boxes and 24. In all: 4 boxes and 36 = 140. So 4 boxes = 104 and 1 box = 26. Lily 26, Kumar 38, Aisha 76.',
 'L3U04-G02': 'Red is 4 boxes and blue is 1 box. After selling 18 red, red is 1 box. So 3 boxes are 18 and 1 box is 6. At first: 6 blue and 24 red, 30 balloons.',
 'L3U04-S04': 'Before: 64 - 29 = 35. After: Siti has 76 and Wei has 41, and 76 - 41 = 35. The gap stays the same because both get the same number of beads.',
 'L3U04-S09': 'First add what she spends: 18.40 + 6.75 = 25.15. Then 50.00 - 25.15 = 24.85.',
 'L3U04-S12': 'In 7 weeks she saves 7 × $12 = $84. Then 84 - 35 = 49: she has $49.',
 'L3U05-C02': '40 ÷ 9 = 4 remainder 4. The 4 people left over need another bus: 5 buses.',
 'L3U05-C04': 'The numbers from 20 to 30 with remainder 2 when divided by 3 are 20, 23, 26 and 29. Only 29 leaves remainder 4 when divided by 5 (25 + 4).',
 'L3U05-G01': '(a) 3 + 5 = 8, and 8 ÷ 7 leaves 1. (b) 3 + 3 = 6, smaller than 7. (c) 5 + 5 = 10, and 10 ÷ 8 leaves 2. Test: 10 + 5 = 15 leaves 1; 20 leaves 6; 26 ÷ 8 leaves 2.',
 'L3U05-S09': '52 ÷ 9 = 5 remainder 7. 5 full buses are not enough, as 7 children are left. So they need 6 buses. The last bus has 7 children, and 9 - 7 = 2 seats are empty.',
 'L3U05-S10': 'The pattern repeats every 4 letters. 30 ÷ 4 = 7 remainder 2, so the 30th letter is the 2nd: B. 32 ÷ 4 = 8 with none left, so the 32nd is the last: D.',
 'L3U05-S13': '47 ÷ 4 = 11 remainder 3. 47 ÷ 5 = 9 remainder 2. 47 ÷ 6 = 7 remainder 5. 47 ÷ 7 = 6 remainder 5. 47 ÷ 8 = 5 remainder 7. The greatest is 7, from 8.',
 'L3U05-T02': 'Numbers with remainder 2 when divided by 5 end in 2 or 7. 67 = 66 + 1 (66 is in the 3 times table) and 67 = 64 + 3 (64 is in the 4 times table). No other.',
 'L3U06-S02': 'The bottom number is the number of equal parts in the whole. The top number is the number of shaded parts. A: 3 of 8. B: 4 of 6. C: 7 of 10. D: 2 of 5.',
 'L3U06-S04': '(a) Same bottom number: 5 parts are more than 3. (b) Thirds are larger than quarters. (c) 2/4 = 4/8. (d) 3/4 = 9/12 and 5/6 = 10/12, and 9 is less than 10.',
 'L3U06-S06': 'When the bottom numbers are the same, add or subtract the top numbers and keep the bottom number. (d) 1 whole is 10/10, so 10/10 - 3/10 = 7/10.',
 'L3U06-T03': '1/4 = 3/12 and 1/3 = 4/12. (a) 3/12 + 4/12 = 7/12. (b) 12/12 - 7/12 = 5/12.',
 'L3U07-S03': '(a) 4 × 7 = 28. (b) 7 × 7 = 49. (c) 4 equal sides: 36 ÷ 4 = 9 m.',
 'L3U07-S11': '(a) 8 × 8 = 64, so each side is 8 m. (b) The border is the perimeter: 32 m.',
 'L3U07-T01': 'Each rectangle is 8 cm by 4 cm: perimeter 24 cm, so 48 cm in all. The square has 32 cm, and 48 - 32 = 16. The 8 cm cut is on both pieces: 16 cm.',
 'L3U07-T02': 'Length + width is 26 ÷ 2 = 13. The pairs that multiply to 36: 1 and 36, 2 and 18, 3 and 12, 4 and 9, 6 and 6. The pair that adds up to 13 is 4 and 9.',
 'L3U08-S04': 'There are 3 small angles. The next size is 2 small angles side by side: 2 of those. Then 1 angle made of all 3 small ones. 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 angles.',
 'L3U09-S01': '(a) 3 × 100 = 300. (b) 4 kg = 4000 g, 4000 + 50 = 4050. (c) 5 ℓ = 5000 ml, 5000 + 300 = 5300. (d) 2000 g is 2 kg, 750 g left. (e) 300 cm is 3 m, 65 cm left.',
 'L3U09-S07': '(a) 6.20 + 4.85 = 11.05, and 11.05 + 3.40 = 14.45. (b) 20.00 - 14.45 = 5.55. (c) 14.45 + 1.75 = 16.20, which is less than $20. So she can.',
 'L3U09-T02': 'From 18:35 to 19:00 is 25 min. 19:00 to 20:00 is 1 h. 20:00 to 20:10 is 10 min. The whole time is 1 h 35 min. Take away the 15 min break: 1 h 20 min.',
 'L3U10-S02': 'The labelled lines go up in 20s and a thin line is halfway between, so each thin line is 10. A is 70, B is 110, C is 90. Together: 70 + 110 + 90 = 270.',
 'L3U10-S03': '(a) 3rd to 24th is 21 days = 3 weeks. So the 24th is a Tuesday again. (b) 3rd to 30th is 27 days = 3 weeks and 6 days. 6 days after Tuesday is Monday.',
 'L3U10-S04': 'Only Lily has a 4-letter name: orange. Kumar has neither apple nor banana: grapes. Wei does not have the apple: banana. Aisha has the apple.',
 'L3U10-S07': 'Each new hexagon adds 5 sticks: 1: 6, 2: 11, 3: 16, 4: 21, 5: 26, 6: 31. For 41 sticks, count on: 7 hexagons need 36 and 8 hexagons need 41.',
 'L3U01-S13': 'First 7: hundreds, 700. Second 7: ones, 7. 700 - 7 = 693.',
 'L3U01-C03': 'The first digit cannot be 0, so use 1, then 0, 5, 6. The number is 1056.',
 'L3U01-S08': '(a) Add 300 each time. (b) Subtract 250 each time. (c) Add 8: 38, then 46.',
 'L3U04-S01': 'The whole bar is 2400 eggs. One part is 875: 2400 - 875 = 1525.',
 'L3U04-S02': '(a) Aisha has fewer: 1450 - 380 = 1070. (b) Together: 1450 + 1070 = 2520.',
 'L3U08-S07': 'Turning right from north: east, south, west. (a) 1 turn: east. (b) 3 turns: west. (c) 2 turns left: west, then south. Two right angles either way: south.',
 'L3U09-T03': 'The 2 notebooks cost $3 more than 2 pens (2 × $1.50). Take that away: $9 - $3 = $6 for 5 pens. A pen costs $6 ÷ 5 = $1.20. A notebook costs $1.20 + $1.50 = $2.70.',
 'L3U10-T03': 'Monday is 1 box, Tuesday 1 box and 15, Wednesday 2 boxes, Thursday 2 boxes take away 10. Total: 6 boxes + 5 = 185, so 1 box = 30. Monday 30, Tuesday 45, Wednesday 60, Thursday 50.',
 'L3U04-G03': 'In the example, take away the 12: 60 - 12 = 48, 48 ÷ 2 = 24. Ali has 24 and Ben has 36.',
 'L3U05-S12': 'A number leaves remainder 3 when it is 3 more than a number in the 5 times table: 18 = 15 + 3, 23 = 20 + 3, 33 = 30 + 3, 48 = 45 + 3. 41 = 40 + 1 leaves 1.',
 'L3U10-S06': 'Fix the drink and list the snacks in order: milk bun, milk pie, milk fruit, then tea bun, tea pie, tea fruit. 3 meals have milk. A bun or a pie: milk bun, milk pie, tea bun, tea pie, so 4.',
 'L3U09-S04': '3 ℓ 200 ml = 3200 ml. 3200 ÷ 500 = 6 remainder 200. She fills 6 pots, with 200 ml left.',
 'L3U08-T02': 'AB goes 2 across for every 1 up. A perpendicular line goes 1 across and 2 down. From C, keep going 1 across and 2 down until you reach the bottom row: R.',
 'L3U02-T03': 'The middle number is in 4 lines (middle row, middle column, 2 diagonals). Add these 4 lines: every number counts once and the middle counts 3 more times. All the numbers make 3 totals (3 rows). So 4 totals = 3 totals + 3 × the middle, and 1 total = 3 × 210 = 630. Then A = 630 - 120 - 270 = 240 and E = 630 - 120 - 210 = 300.',
 'L3U07-C01': 'Area is length × width: 9 × 5 = 45 cm².',
 'L3U10-S03': '(a) 3rd to 24th is 21 days = 3 weeks. So the 24th is a Tuesday again. (b) 3rd to 30th is 27 days = 3 weeks and 6 days. 6 days after Tuesday is Monday.',
}
